WEBVTT

00:00:01.628 --> 00:00:04.320
- So this is camera space.

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:06.300
Now, it always confuses me,
camera space,

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:07.840
because there's no camera.

00:00:07.840 --> 00:00:09.376
Where is it?

00:00:09.376 --> 00:00:10.960
What's going on?

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:12.520
- Of course,
there's no camera.

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:15.175
The camera is actually 
your eyeball.

00:00:15.175 --> 00:00:17.655
And so you're looking
at a computer screen.

00:00:17.655 --> 00:00:20.375
And as you look at 
the computer screen,

00:00:20.375 --> 00:00:23.128
your're looking from your
eye through the screen.

00:00:23.128 --> 00:00:24.735
All right?

00:00:24.735 --> 00:00:27.227
And then you're looking out
into the expanse of the world.

00:00:27.227 --> 00:00:28.880
- Where is the camera then?

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:30.800
- Your eye.
- My eye. Okay.

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:33.100
- Your eye is at the origin,
and we are looking

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:35.893
down the
negative "zed" axis.

00:00:35.893 --> 00:00:38.858
- Oh, you said it right.
"Zed." I like that.

00:00:38.858 --> 00:00:40.398
- I can adapt.

00:00:40.398 --> 00:00:42.395
So I'm looking down
the negative Z axis.

00:00:42.395 --> 00:00:44.823
I'm going to be looking
through the rectangle

00:00:44.823 --> 00:00:47.633
that is the front screen,
and then pretending

00:00:47.633 --> 00:00:51.319
that there's a whole
expanse universe behind it.

00:00:51.319 --> 00:00:52.933
- Now, you said the word origin.

00:00:52.933 --> 00:00:54.462
- Yeah.
- What's that?

00:00:54.462 --> 00:00:56.156
- That's the centre
of the universe,

00:00:56.156 --> 00:00:57.710
which is my eyeball.

00:00:57.710 --> 00:00:59.608
- Oh, so each person
is their own centre

00:00:59.608 --> 00:01:01.129
of the universe?
- Yeah, yeah.

00:01:01.129 --> 00:01:02.990
In your favorite camera space,

00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:04.857
what we're looking at then is

00:01:04.857 --> 00:01:06.952
gazing out through the screen

00:01:06.952 --> 00:01:09.458
into space, and the camera

00:01:09.458 --> 00:01:11.068
does not move because

00:01:11.068 --> 00:01:12.885
the person
doesn't move.

00:01:12.885 --> 00:01:14.844
So a person just sits there

00:01:14.844 --> 00:01:16.885
and sits in front of
the computer screen.

00:01:16.885 --> 00:01:18.996
And, for example, if I said,

00:01:18.996 --> 00:01:21.525
look to your left,

00:01:21.525 --> 00:01:24.099
you might swing around 
and look left,

00:01:24.099 --> 00:01:26.165
but the camera is 
actually not moving.

00:01:26.165 --> 00:01:27.903
So what has to happen is
the whole universe

00:01:27.903 --> 00:01:29.530
then has to pan to the right.

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:31.753
- The whole...
Well, maybe we can ask Cassidy.

00:01:31.753 --> 00:01:34.035
You you want to try this?
Like, looking to the left.

00:01:34.035 --> 00:01:35.578
- Let's try it.
- Okay, let's...

00:01:35.578 --> 00:01:37.375
- So, Cassidy, look
to the...

00:01:37.375 --> 00:01:40.090
Look to the left.

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:42.795
- Okay, so we're looking to
the left of the coffee shop.

00:01:42.795 --> 00:01:44.295
- Yeah, but are you seeing that
the whole coffee shop

00:01:44.295 --> 00:01:47.186
is moving to the right?
- Oh, right.

00:01:47.186 --> 00:01:49.675
Yeah, the coffee shop
did go across this way.

00:01:49.675 --> 00:01:51.551
- Yeah.
- Huh.

00:01:51.551 --> 00:01:53.115
That's just like
looking left and right.

00:01:53.115 --> 00:01:56.017
How about moving left and right?
Like, changing position.

00:01:56.017 --> 00:01:57.551
- Same kind of thing.
We're going to...

00:01:57.551 --> 00:01:59.065
The camera doesn't move.

00:01:59.065 --> 00:02:02.270
The camera is at the origin.
It is your eyeball.

00:02:02.270 --> 00:02:03.977
You're looking at a computer 
screen,

00:02:03.977 --> 00:02:06.372
and if you pan to the right,

00:02:06.372 --> 00:02:08.598
the universe is going to
shift to the left.

00:02:08.598 --> 00:02:10.131
- Does it also work

00:02:10.131 --> 00:02:12.185
backwards, forwards,
up, down?

00:02:12.185 --> 00:02:13.701
- Everything.

00:02:13.701 --> 00:02:15.532
There is no camera.

00:02:15.532 --> 00:02:17.994
The camera is your eyeball
at the origin

00:02:17.994 --> 00:02:19.506
looking into a computer screen.

00:02:19.506 --> 00:02:21.042
That's all there is to it.

00:02:21.042 --> 00:02:22.910
- So instead of me
walking around,

00:02:22.910 --> 00:02:25.240
the universe is moving?

00:02:25.240 --> 00:02:26.995
- Now, that's how
the math works out

00:02:26.995 --> 00:02:29.282
in order to get these kind of
projections to work.

00:02:29.282 --> 00:02:31.766
However, this will drive
everybody else crazy.

00:02:31.766 --> 00:02:34.348
People want to be able
to say there is a camera.

00:02:34.348 --> 00:02:36.618
So what we'll have to do
mathematically is

00:02:36.618 --> 00:02:39.580
allow for that by
shifting the universe

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:41.958
to the left when you say

00:02:41.958 --> 00:02:43.925
rotate right 
or look to the right.

00:02:43.925 --> 00:02:46.248
And so I'll just put a little
minus sign on that thing,

00:02:46.248 --> 00:02:47.741
flip it around the other way,

00:02:47.741 --> 00:02:49.851
and now all the art people
and film people

00:02:49.851 --> 00:02:51.441
and all that,
they're all content.

00:02:51.441 --> 00:02:54.380
They think everything is
working like it's a camera.

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:56.520
But I had to set up
the math to do that.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:58.764
- So I remember you explaining
this to a colleague of ours.

00:02:58.764 --> 00:03:01.017
You were like, yeah,
it's the whole universe

00:03:01.017 --> 00:03:03.580
that moves, not the camera.

00:03:03.580 --> 00:03:05.827
And they were like, oh,
that that seems like

00:03:05.827 --> 00:03:08.193
a lot of computations
to move the universe.

00:03:08.193 --> 00:03:09.835
- You know, all of
this is make believe.

00:03:09.835 --> 00:03:11.799
None of this is real,
so it doesn't cost you

00:03:11.799 --> 00:03:13.644
more energy to move 
the universe

00:03:13.644 --> 00:03:15.163
than it does to move the camera.

00:03:15.163 --> 00:03:16.685
That's absolutely crazy.

00:03:16.685 --> 00:03:19.035
The camera doesn't move.

00:03:19.035 --> 00:03:21.222
It looks down
the negative Z axis

00:03:21.222 --> 00:03:23.347
through the computer monitor,

00:03:23.347 --> 00:03:25.338
and what we see out there

00:03:25.338 --> 00:03:26.940
is what you see.

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:29.660
And you're at the origin.
So stop it.

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:31.926
There is no camera.

00:03:31.926 --> 00:03:33.743
- So if I remember correctly,

00:03:33.743 --> 00:03:36.165
you have spoiled
Super Mario Kart

00:03:36.165 --> 00:03:38.307
for one of our colleagues.

00:03:38.307 --> 00:03:41.126
- A lot of people can't play
first person shooters anymore

00:03:41.126 --> 00:03:43.585
after I explained all this
because he was playing this game

00:03:43.585 --> 00:03:46.835
and he made it look like the...

00:03:46.835 --> 00:03:49.567
that the cart is racing
around this track

00:03:49.567 --> 00:03:51.383
when, in fact,

00:03:51.383 --> 00:03:53.700
the camera doesn't move,
the cart didn't really move.

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:55.220
The cart moved back and forth.

00:03:55.220 --> 00:03:57.392
From the viewer's perspective,

00:03:57.392 --> 00:04:00.667
the cart goes back and forth,
but really what we were doing,

00:04:00.667 --> 00:04:04.333
we're dragging the racetrack
underneath you.

00:04:04.333 --> 00:04:06.482
So you are holding still
and the racetrack moves,

00:04:06.482 --> 00:04:09.088
moves along because
you do not move.

00:04:09.088 --> 00:04:10.855
The camera does not move.

00:04:10.855 --> 00:04:12.505
The camera is at the origin

00:04:12.505 --> 00:04:15.311
looking down the
negative zed axis.

00:04:15.311 --> 00:04:17.133
People who play first-person
shooters get upset

00:04:17.133 --> 00:04:19.202
when I explain this fact to them

00:04:19.202 --> 00:04:21.681
because they'll look to the left
and they'll see the whole world

00:04:21.681 --> 00:04:24.610
shifting to the right and
they go, wow, what happened?

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:26.604
And then boof, they got shot.
Okay?

00:04:26.604 --> 00:04:28.268
Because they're not
paying attention.

00:04:28.268 --> 00:04:30.102
It's crazy stuff.
It's very easy.

00:04:30.102 --> 00:04:31.981
- Now, you mentioned
looking down negative Z.

00:04:31.981 --> 00:04:33.490
Why is that so important?

00:04:33.490 --> 00:04:35.845
Why is that way negative Z?

00:04:35.845 --> 00:04:38.485
- You know, it really
is kind of arbitrary.

00:04:38.485 --> 00:04:40.690
In some things, they
they put Z as going up.

00:04:40.690 --> 00:04:42.753
I mean, why negative Z

00:04:42.753 --> 00:04:44.485
into the camera?

00:04:44.485 --> 00:04:46.924
Why not? And it's probably 
based on the right hand rule

00:04:46.924 --> 00:04:48.897
or left hand rule.
- Oh, yes. The right hand.

00:04:48.897 --> 00:04:51.488
If my thumb points

00:04:51.488 --> 00:04:53.205
to the right,

00:04:53.205 --> 00:04:55.710
that's X,

00:04:55.710 --> 00:04:57.790
up for me is Y.

00:04:57.790 --> 00:05:00.482
And my middle finger points
towards you,

00:05:00.482 --> 00:05:03.288
points towards the camera,
that's positive Z.

00:05:03.288 --> 00:05:05.805
So the negative Z
would be getting...

00:05:05.805 --> 00:05:07.641
- It goes into
the computer screen

00:05:07.641 --> 00:05:09.214
from the right hand rule.

00:05:09.214 --> 00:05:12.125
- So I know we argue a lot
about camera space.

00:05:12.125 --> 00:05:14.403
I do a lot of my math
in world space

00:05:14.403 --> 00:05:17.165
where the physics is,
but I have to confess.

00:05:17.165 --> 00:05:19.850
I have an assignment,
a snooker assignment.

00:05:19.850 --> 00:05:22.330
I say you pronounce 
it right. Yeah. Snooker.

00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:25.199
So, yeah,
when you play snooker,

00:05:25.199 --> 00:05:27.914
you want to hit the ball

00:05:27.914 --> 00:05:29.513
to where you're looking,

00:05:29.513 --> 00:05:31.210
so into negative Z.

00:05:31.210 --> 00:05:33.449
So it's actually
really handy to do

00:05:33.449 --> 00:05:36.089
the snooker,

00:05:36.089 --> 00:05:38.255
the cue motion in camera space.

00:05:38.255 --> 00:05:40.481
So I'm always hitting
the ball in negative Z,

00:05:40.481 --> 00:05:42.829
the camera space,
and then I do some math

00:05:42.829 --> 00:05:45.093
to get it back in
the world space.

00:05:45.093 --> 00:05:47.312
- Yeah, what's crazy is that
you were talking about

00:05:47.312 --> 00:05:49.661
the model space earlier on.

00:05:49.661 --> 00:05:53.143
And in model space, when I'm
setting up the universe,

00:05:53.143 --> 00:05:55.870
I'm not going to
move the camera back.

00:05:55.870 --> 00:05:58.157
I'm going to push the object

00:05:58.157 --> 00:06:01.495
into negative "zee" space.

00:06:01.495 --> 00:06:04.155
So I'm pushing the whole scene
out in front of me because,

00:06:04.155 --> 00:06:05.735
again, the camera doesn't move.

00:06:05.735 --> 00:06:07.380
It sits at the origin
looking down

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:08.961
the negative "zed" axis.

00:06:08.961 --> 00:06:11.848
So my first move with the...

00:06:11.848 --> 00:06:13.539
with the view matrix would be

00:06:13.539 --> 00:06:15.260
to shove everything down

00:06:15.260 --> 00:06:18.309
the negative "zee" axis.
- The view matrix, right.

00:06:18.309 --> 00:06:20.518
So now I've got 
to think about spaces.

00:06:20.518 --> 00:06:23.654
Okay, so we've had Paul Neale
talking about

00:06:23.654 --> 00:06:26.183
model space.

00:06:26.183 --> 00:06:28.507
Then world space.
Okay. I'm on.

00:06:28.507 --> 00:06:30.205
Now we're on camera space.

00:06:30.205 --> 00:06:32.860
So to go from world to camera

00:06:32.860 --> 00:06:35.043
would be the view matrix?

00:06:35.043 --> 00:06:36.563
Okay. Right.

00:06:36.563 --> 00:06:38.445
And sometimes I 
have to go the other way.

00:06:38.445 --> 00:06:40.525
Like, in one of my assignments,

00:06:40.525 --> 00:06:42.790
you're clicking on
objects in the world.

00:06:42.790 --> 00:06:44.852
So you got the mouse position

00:06:44.852 --> 00:06:46.778
in screen space,
and I'm coming

00:06:46.778 --> 00:06:48.813
all the way back
to world space.

00:06:48.813 --> 00:06:51.830
So I use the inverse
of the view matrix.

00:06:51.830 --> 00:06:53.830
- Yeah, I think we set
that up for you one day.

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:55.823
- Yeah.
- Moving all the way back

00:06:55.823 --> 00:06:58.198
so we get from the screen

00:06:58.198 --> 00:06:59.867
back into...

00:06:59.867 --> 00:07:01.405
did you click on the model?

00:07:01.405 --> 00:07:03.963
- Yes, all the way
to model space.

00:07:03.963 --> 00:07:06.208
Okay, so if we can
summarize this,

00:07:06.208 --> 00:07:07.950
this is camera space.

00:07:07.950 --> 00:07:09.984
I like to call it
Scott Fielder space

00:07:09.984 --> 00:07:12.304
because we argue
about this so much,

00:07:12.304 --> 00:07:14.590
but it's all just a matter
of perspective, isn't it?

00:07:14.590 --> 00:07:16.180
Am I moving

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:17.710
or is the universe moving?

00:07:17.710 --> 00:07:19.258
- The universe moves.
- Oh, okay. There we go.

00:07:19.258 --> 00:07:21.165
- There's no argument.
- So, okay.

00:07:21.165 --> 00:07:23.335
So that means the
camera is at the origin.

00:07:23.335 --> 00:07:25.045
- Yeah.
- That means X is zero,

00:07:25.045 --> 00:07:26.561
Y is zero,

00:07:26.561 --> 00:07:28.684
Z is zero.
- Exactly.

00:07:28.684 --> 00:07:31.965
Never changes, and we're
looking down negative zed.

00:07:31.965 --> 00:07:33.923
- Yep, and if you do that,

00:07:33.923 --> 00:07:35.923
your math will be easier.